      "claim": "Ampicillin, sharing the alpha-aminoacyl dipeptide-like pharmacophore but with a penam core, had variable oral bioavailability of about 29-62% in humans; nearly all values remain above the 20% threshold and therefore support the pass prediction, though with less direct transferability than cephradine or amoxicillin.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive",
      "molecule_ids": ["Molecule 9", "Molecule 10"],
      "record_ids": ["Record 9-1", "Record 10-1", "Record 10-2", "Record 10-3", "Record 10-4", "Record 10-5", "Record 10-6", "Record 10-7", "Record 10-8", "Record 10-9", "Record 10-10"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Cefazolin and ceftriaxone have very low oral bioavailability (1.5-2% and 8%, respectively), but they lack the alpha-amino dipeptide-like intestinal transporter motif present in the query and are structurally distant, with very low transfer scores; this is only weak contradictory evidence and does not override the strong analogous high-bioavailability data.",
